PlayGo chunk-hash over-allocation fix for large (multi-GB) packages.

LibOrbisPkg sizes the PLAYGO_CHUNK_SHA entry from an *estimate* of the package
size, but the *final* package_size ends up larger due to alignment overhead. For
multi-GB packages (any normal PS2 ISO) the required hash table is then bigger
than the reserved space and the build aborts with:

    Playgo Chunk hash file was not allocated enough space. Report this as a bug

The final DataSize is reset to the exact required size later in BuildPkg, so
over-allocating the estimate only leaves harmless padding. We bump the reserve
by 0x10000 (covers ~1 GB of estimate drift; real drift is a few KB).

File: LibOrbisPkg/PKG/PkgBuilder.cs  (in BuildPkg, the `entry == pkg.ChunkSha` branch)

--- a/LibOrbisPkg/PKG/PkgBuilder.cs
+++ b/LibOrbisPkg/PKG/PkgBuilder.cs
@@
           // Add the size of the chunk SHAs, plus an extra 16 bytes for good measure
           pkgSize += ((pkgSize + 16) / 0x10000) * 4;
-          e.DataSize = (uint)(pkgSize / 0x10000L) * 4;
+          // Over-allocate by 64KB so the estimate never falls below the final
+          // package_size for large packages (see patches/ note). DataSize is reset
+          // to the exact size later, so the extra reservation is harmless padding.
+          e.DataSize = (uint)(pkgSize / 0x10000L) * 4 + 0x10000;

setup.sh applies this transformation idempotently with sed; this file documents
the change for humans and for upstreaming.
